Q: Is there an equivalent of Spring Retry&#39;s Sleeper bean in Spring Core? A: <p>The logic in the new <code>RetryTemplate</code> is like this:</p>
<pre><code> try {
                long sleepTime = backOffExecution.nextBackOff();
                if (sleepTime == BackOffExecution.STOP) {
                    break;
                }
                checkIfTimeoutExceeded(timeout, startTime, sleepTime, retryable, retryState);
                logger.debug(() -&gt; &quot;Backing off for %dms after retryable operation '%s'&quot;
                        .formatted(sleepTime, retryableName));
                Thread.sleep(sleepTime);
    }
</code></pre>
<p>That <code>BackOffExecution</code> is based on respective options from the <code>RetryPolicy.Builder</code>.</p>
<p>In your case you can do something like this:</p>
<pre><code>RetryTemplate rtt = new RetryTemplate();
rtt.setRetryPolicy(RetryPolicy.builder().maxRetries(2).delay(Duration.ZERO).build());
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>delay(Duration.ZERO)</code> is what you want to make it not sleeping too much between retries.</p>

Q: I made a simple crash algorithm, can you take a look at it? A: <p>Here is some code that would actually measure the code of a performant collision detector.</p>
<pre><code>void test()
{
    float mindist2 = 16;            // minimum distance squared
    std::vector&lt;float&gt; vx, vy;      // random obstacles
    float x,y;                      // test object
    std::vector&lt;std::pair&lt;float,float&gt;&gt; 
        vcollisions;                // colliding obstacles


    // ... populate vx, vy


    float* px = vx.data();
    float* py = vy.data();
    for( int k = 0; k &lt; vx.size(); k++ )
    {
        float dx = x - *px;
        float dy = y - *py;
        if(( dx*dx + dy*dy ) &lt; mindist2 )
            vcollisions.emplace_back(*px,*py);
        px++;
        py++;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to create a member function for map? A: <p>IMO best way to approach this is to encapsulate this complex data structure in some class.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class Example {
public:
    void SetValue(const std::string&amp; a, const std::string&amp; b, int i, int value) {
        example[a][b][i].push_back(value);
    }
private:
    std::map&lt;std::string, std::map&lt;string, std::map&lt;int, std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; example;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Pros:</p>
<ul>
<li>autocomplete will have no problem with this approach</li>
<li>data structure is isolated from rest of the code. If you decide to change this data structure to something more optimized, you do not have to hunt all use of this class just update implementation of <code>SetValue</code> and other required member functions.</li>
<li>easier to read and maintain</li>
<li>no macros</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>Depending on context there are other solutions. For example introduce some complex type for a key:</p>
<pre><code>class MyKey {
public:
    MyKey(const std::string&amp; a, const std::string&amp; b, int i) : a{a}, b{b}, i{i}
    {}

    auto operator&lt;=&gt;(const MyKey &amp;) const noexcept = default;

private:
    std::string a;
    std::string b;
    int i;

    friend struct std::hash&lt;MyKey&gt;;
};

template&lt;&gt;
struct std::hash&lt;MyKey&gt; {
    std::size_t operator()(const MyKey&amp; k) const noexcept {
        std::size_t h1 = std::hash&lt;std::string&gt;{}(k.a);
        std::size_t h2 = std::hash&lt;std::string&gt;{}(k.b);
        std::size_t h3 = std::hash&lt;int&gt;{}(k.i);

        std::size_t seed = h1;
        seed ^= h2 + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL + (seed &lt;&lt; 6) + (seed &gt;&gt; 2);
        seed ^= h3 + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL + (seed &lt;&lt; 6) + (seed &gt;&gt; 2);
        return seed;
    }
};

class Example {
public:
    void SetValue(const std::string&amp; a, const std::string&amp; b, int i, int value) {
        SetValue({a, b, i}, value));
    }
    void SetValue(const MyKey&amp; key, int value) {
        example[key].push_back(value);
    }
private:
    std::unordered_map&lt;MyKey, std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; example;
};
</code></pre>
<p>This is a bit more advanced, but IMO you should get familiar with such solution.</p>
